# Draft: kk: support VK_EXT_vertex_input_dynamic_state
# https://gitlab.freedesktop.org/mesa/mesa/-/merge_requests/45831 state=opened
# This series implements `VK_EXT_vertex_input_dynamic_state` in KosmicKrisp using per-layout vertex input prologs linked into the render pipeline through `MTL4StaticLinkingDescriptor`. For each layout, the driver builds small prolog functions with the attribute formats, input rates, and divisors baked in as constants, while offsets and strides remain in the root descriptor table so layouts that differ only in those can share a pipeline.

To avoid stalling command recording when a new layout is first used, specialized pipelines compile on a background device queue while draws fall back to a generic linked pipeline compiled up front. For deterministic testing, `MESA_KK_DEBUG=vi_prolog_sync` waits for the specialized pipeline and `MESA_KK_DEBUG=vi_prolog_generic` keeps using the generic one. Vertex shaders emulated as compute before tessellation or geometry are compiled ahead of time and define the generic prologs inline.

The branch is split into six bisectable commits covering the NIR intrinsic, Metal bridge linking, compiler support, prolog fetch, background compilation, and a final four-line commit advertising the extension. If you prefer to review and land the first five commits as groundwork ahead of `VK_EXT_shader_object`, I am happy to hold back the final commit.

Tested on an Apple A18 Pro running macOS 27.2. On the Vulkan CTS vertex-input and robustness groups, the series adds 556 new passes with no regressions against `main` (results are attached in the comments). In VS-bound draws from 1 to 16 attributes, fetch cost stays within 1% of static vertex input; the main tradeoff is compiling the generic pipeline at creation time (258 ms cold at 16 attributes, compared with 148 ms for five static pipelines).

All commits carry `Generated-by: LLM`.
